 "productionNotes": "No single production total is given because the 4-4-2 was an option package for five of its nine model years and the sources consulted do not cover every year. What can be traced: 2,999 for 1964, a figure that appears in Wikipedia and in pages that restate it, so it rests on one chain of citation rather than several; 25,003 for 1965, including 3,468 convertibles; 21,997 for 1966; and for 1967 either 24,833 (Wikipedia and musclecarfacts) or 24,829 (Silodrome), a four-car difference that no source here resolves. For 1968 the only figure fetched is 'over 33,000'. No 1969 or 1971 total was found in a source retrieved for this page. For 1970 the Over-Drive Magazine fact sheet gives 14,709 Holiday hardtops, 2,933 convertibles and 1,688 sport coupes, which add to 19,330, with 3,100 W-30 cars among them; Lang Collections separately counts 264 W-30 convertibles that year. For 1972 the 4-4-2 was the W-29 package with no VIN code of its own, so no 4-4-2 total is published in the sources here, only the W-30 count of 659 coupes and 113 convertibles. The W-30 counts by year are the most-quoted rarity figures: 54 factory cars plus 97 dealer installations in 1966, 502 factory engines for NHRA homologation in 1967, 3,100 in 1970 and 772 in 1972. Hurst/Olds production is 515 for 1968 (459 Holiday coupes, 56 sport coupes) and 906 Holiday coupes plus six prototypes and two convertibles for 1969, though the Hurst/Olds is a separate model and not counted as a 4-4-2. Several of these figures trace back to Oldsmobile's own records through secondary writers; no Oldsmobile production document was retrieved directly for this page.",
